module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { NoRungStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a wall carries a non-emptiness conjunct over a derived population — `length(xs: roster()) > 0`, a `count > 0`, a `both sides present` pair — whose CLASS has no producer anywhere in the tree. The class is not merely unpopulated today; nothing constructs it. The wall is therefore not red-as-in-broken and not red-as-in-awaiting-a-row: it is unsatisfiable until a producer exists, and it will keep reading as slop debt in the ledger until someone either manufactures a specimen to satisfy it or identifies the missing PRODUCER as the actual trigger.",

    "HARM, AND IT IS NOT THE RED. The red is honest. The harm is the misfiled trigger and the two wrong repairs it invites. (1) DELETING THE CONJUNCT to go green, which deletes the discrimination too: a wall that no longer requires the class to be inhabited also stops noticing when the class is emptied by accident, so the next real regression in that classification is silent. (2) MANUFACTURING A SPECIMEN to satisfy the conjunct — a row written to have the shape the roster filter tests, which turns a claim about the compiler's behaviour into a claim about a literal, and goes green while the behaviour it was written to pin down does not exist. Both repairs look like closing the debt; both destroy the witness.",

    "WHERE THE RED SITS, AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT RE-DERIVES IT. Two witnesses of test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness fail a non-emptiness conjunct: no_batch_mixes_a_pre_verdict_row_with_an_assertion_false_row fails its final `length(xs: pre_verdict_sides) > 0`, and both_red_expectations_are_known_red_but_only_one_expects_a_pre_verdict_refusal fails `pre_verdict_count > 0` over the same population. Both counts come from gunbc.explicit_witness_admission known_red_pre_verdict_refusal_roster, which filters the data explicit_witness_admissions ledger for rows whose expectation is ExpectTypedPreVerdictRefusal; no row in that ledger declares it, and the row constructor known_red_pre_verdict_probe has no call site anywhere. RE-DERIVE by reading the three cited ledger declarations in `evidence` — the roster's filter predicate, the ledger itself, and the unused constructor — or by running the instrument below over the two cited witness declarations.",

    "THE PRODUCER DOES NOT EXIST, AND THAT IS THE TRIGGER TO NAME. Nothing classifies a probe as stopping before a verdict. `expect_pre_verdict_refusal` has no definition in .dag or .rs, and the pre-verdict vocabulary (PreVerdictResolve, PreVerdictCodegen, UnboundImportedVariantConstructor) appears only on the CLAIM side — std.witness_admission's expected-verdict-split note, gunbc.explicit_witness_admission's row constructors, and test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness. There is no classifier on the probe side. The last one was Rust: `PreVerdictPhase` and `PreVerdictCodegenError`, deleted 2026-10-14 by #13861 (29a76c8a8b, `Delete unreached claim_executor code`). Since then the vocabulary has had a consumer and no producer, which is the fact §6b makes the trigger: the fix is not a ledger edit, it is a probe host that can report a refusal cause from BEFORE the verdict phase. RE-DERIVE the absence mechanically: `git log --all -S'expect_pre_verdict_refusal'` finds no definition, and `git log -S'PreVerdictPhase' -- '*.rs'` ends at 29a76c8a8b.",

    "HOW THE EMPTY CLASS WAS PROVEN EMPTY RATHER THAN MERELY UNLISTED, AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT RE-PROVES IT. Every row the well-formedness wall refused (gunbc.explicit_witness_admission explicit_witness_admission_well_formed — sixteen QuarantineProbeExpectRed rows plus the two constructor_tag_route_probe_red rows still live at the time) was probed individually, one function per run. Each reported the per-function FALSE arm rather than the resolve-error arm, and that distinction is the whole claim: a witness whose entry never resolves cannot be a pre-verdict specimen, and only a host that classified a stop before the verdict could produce one. THE INSTRUMENT IS THE `claim_batch` BINARY — `cargo build --release -p v1-compiler --bin claim_batch`, then `claim_batch --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry <witness .dag path> --functions <fn>` — and the arm is selected by report_outcome in src/v1/stage0/src/bin/claim_batch.rs, with the resolve-error arm produced by resolve_timed; a reader re-derives the result by running that binary per row, not by trusting this prose. The same probe on the deleted last specimen (the logic_ground_truth trio, removed 2026-08-17 by #8283 / 611fd02770, restored verbatim from `git show 611fd02770^`) took the resolve-error arm: it dies on its own imports, so it is a resolve failure and cannot be enrolled as a pre-verdict row either. The honest disposition is red-until-the-producer-exists, which is what this row carries.",

    "THE FIXTURE VARIANT, for when the red is in a SYNTHETIC claim rather than a real roster. A test module may hold a `data fixture_entry: String = \"some/path.dag\"` pointing at a file that has never existed in any revision, and drive claims over rows built from it. The claim then tests the PREDICATES against a made-up subject and can never fail for the reason it names, because no verifier ever walks the path. A live example, with its reason it cannot simply be deleted: test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness data fixture_entry names dag/test/claim/synthetic_admission_witness_test.dag, a path absent from every revision, and it is consumed by fixture_exact_admission_rows and fixture_file_grain_admission_rows, which in turn feed admission_reaches_the_admitted_function_and_not_its_sibling, admission_does_not_reach_the_same_function_name_in_another_file, and red_control_file_grain_admission_would_reach_the_sibling — deleting the pointer would delete those claims. Where such a pointer has consumers, the repair is the missing fixture file, not the pointer. For accepting the unresolvable-import case itself see gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unused_import_of_a_missing_module_is_accepted — that row is about a dangling IMPORT, which is now mechanically refused; a dangling STRING PATH used as data is a different harm and this is the row for it.",


    "NEIGHBOUR WHOSE CLASS THIS IS NOT. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_possibly_empty_producer_enters_a_nonempty_consumer is the nearest and it is NOT this: its INVALID STATE is a consumer that treats emptiness as impossible and stays SILENT when the population is empty. Here the consumer does the opposite — it refuses loudly and permanently — so nothing is silent and nothing is green. The shared shape is a population and a non-emptiness test; the difference is direction of the failure and, with it, the repair. That row's repair is to widen the consumer; this row's repair is to build the producer or to say the class is empty on purpose. Deleting the test is a repair that fits NEITHER and is the one most often chosen, because it is the only one that goes green today.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.explicit_witness_admission", decl_name: "known_red_pre_verdict_refusal_roster"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.explicit_witness_admission", decl_name: "known_red_pre_verdict_probe"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.explicit_witness_admission", decl_name: "explicit_witness_admissions"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.explicit_witness_admission", decl_name: "explicit_witness_admission_well_formed"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "std.witness_admission", decl_name: "witness_cadence_has_scheduled_route"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness", decl_name: "no_batch_mixes_a_pre_verdict_row_with_an_assertion_false_row"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness", decl_name: "both_red_expectations_are_known_red_but_only_one_expects_a_pre_verdict_refusal"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness", decl_name: "fixture_entry"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness", decl_name: "admission_reaches_the_admitted_function_and_not_its_sibling"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness", decl_name: "red_control_file_grain_admission_would_reach_the_sibling"),
  ],
}
